      <h3 class="mb-2" style="font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: bold;">Why CiteIt.net?</h3>
      <p>Often when I'm reading a story in print, I come across a quote that makes me wonder: What was said in the sentences preceding or following the quote?  In other words: <strong>What is the Context?</strong>  Is this quote cherry-picked and can I really get a full sense for what happened from just the quoted selection?</p>
      <br />

      <h3 class="mb-2" style="font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: bold;">What is CiteIt.net?</h3>
      <p class="mb-5">CiteIt.net is the name I chose for a web service that allows writers to augment their writing with <strong>greater context about the quotations they make</strong>.  The concept behind CiteIt.net is part of the tradition that started with footnotes, became hypercharged with <a href="http://www.w3.org/WhatIs.html">hypertext links</a>, and now has evolved to enable the words of the original sources to flow into the citing document, <strong>without requiring the reader to interrupt their reading experience by leaving the original document</strong>.</p>

      <h3 class="mb-2" style="font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: bold;">Inspiration</h3>
      <p class="mb-5">CiteIt.net was <strong>inspired by</strong> the work of <a class="underline" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ted_Nelson">Ted Nelson</a> &mdash; who coined the term &ldquo;<strong><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypertext" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">hypertext</a></strong>&rdquo; in 1962 and had the vision for a universal hypertext network, long before Bill Gates and Steve Jobs had even assembled their first personal computers.</p>
      <p class="mb-5"><a href="/assets/images/philosophy_of_hypertext.png" title="Full-size Image: Philosophy of Hypertext"><img loading="lazy" class="alignnone size-medium" src="/assets/images/philosophy_of_hypertext.png" alt="Philosophy of Hypertext" style="float:right; padding-left: 20px; padding-bottom: 20px" width="230" height="295" /></a>

      While writing a <a class="underline" href="https://www.openpolitics.com/articles/ted-nelson-philosophy-of-hypertext.html"><strong>review of Ted Nelson&rsquo;s 2002 Ph.D. thesis</strong></a>, I was inspired to develop a way to write in a way more similar to what Nelson advocates.&nbsp; Ted&rsquo;s design &mdash; <a class="underline" href="http://www.xanadu.net/">Xanadu</a> &mdash; features <strong>parallel texts</strong> that provide the full context of their sources. My design is a crude semblance of Xanadu, displaying only one pane of text in which the text surrounding a citation is &ldquo;injected&rdquo;.&nbsp; Because I do <strong>not have the technical ability to implement Ted&rsquo;s vision</strong> and so much existing writing is available on the web as Html, I thought I could pursue Ted&rsquo;s vision best by <strong>creating a quick-and-dirty extension to Html</strong> that would allow writers to start to create a collection of citation data.&nbsp; There is a saying from the 1989 movie &ldquo;Field of Dreams &mdash; <q cite="https://en.wikiquote.org/wiki/Field_of_Dreams" data-citeit-citing-url="/about/">If you build it &ndash;</q> &ndash; he will come. My approach is to get started using a <strong>simplified implementation</strong>, as a foundation that attracts others to the task of building a better version of hypertext.&nbsp; As the system develops, and as authors get used to the concept of pulling their quote context from the original source, it is my hope that the compilation of a public data set of citation data will attract great programmers to gradually rebuild hypertext, <strong>replacing my current Html cludge</strong> with advanced front and backend features.</p>
      <br />
      <p><a href="/assets/images/tim-langeman-2018-06-18-fullsize.jpg" title="Full-size Image: Tim Langeman (2018-06-18)"><img loading="lazy" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-709" src="/assets/images/tim-langeman-2018-06-18-small.jpg" alt="photo: Tim Langeman" width="227" height="300"></a><br>
      &ndash;<a class="underline" href="https://www.openpolitics.com/tim">Tim Langeman</a> &nbsp;&nbsp; (<a class="underline" href="https://www.openpolitics.com/tim">home page</a>)<br>
      Lancaster County, PA (USA)</p>

      <h2 class="mb-2" style="font-size: 1.9em; font-weight: bold;">Benefits:</h2>

      <h3 class="mt-8 mb-4" style="font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: bold;">Writers:</h3>
      <ul class="disc">
        <li>Earn the <strong>trust of potential subscribers</strong> by dispelling skepticism about source context and creditability</li>
        <li>Engage readers' <strong>curiosity</strong> about quoted material — enabling drill down into detail, <strong>without losing readers in separate tabs</strong></li>
        <li>Earn the <strong>trust of interviewees</strong> who know their interview quotes will include the full context</li>
        <li>Writers can use citations to <strong>defend themselves against critics</strong> who question their journalistic credentials or conversely <strong>attack those who cherry-pick their sources</strong></li>
      </ul><br />

      <h3 class="mb-2" style="font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: bold;">Editors:</h3>
      <ul class="disc">
        <li>It acts as a built-in <strong>fact-checking tool</strong> that saves the legal/editorial team time.</li>
      </ul><br />

      <h3 class="mb-2" style="font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: bold;">Readers:</h3>
      <ul class="disc">
        <li>It transforms a static article into an immersive, high-trust research document.</li>
        <li>In addition to <strong>building trust</strong> and engaging <strong>curiosity</strong>, some citations can also provide <strong>entertainment</strong></li>
      </ul>

